WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:06.180
ROBIN KELSEY: So it's interesting with photography

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:09.060
the extent to which some of the great photographers,

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:12.870
particularly the great women photographers of the Victorian era,

00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:16.379
began just with taking the sort of photographs

00:00:16.379 --> 00:00:21.310
that someone would take of family members, and friends, and so forth.

00:00:21.310 --> 00:00:25.380
And it's through that, becoming interested in more

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:27.840
ambitious photography.

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:30.090
And that's the case with Beals.

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:33.030
So it's interesting to look through her photographs

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:37.470
and to see the kinds of exchanges, the intimate exchanges

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:42.330
that photography enabled, taking photographs of loved ones or friends,

00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:45.340
posing, performing for one another.

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:48.960
And these kinds of exchanges within the homes of that era

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:52.740
were a very important part of social life.

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:56.820
What was unusual about Beals is she then took the next step,

00:00:56.820 --> 00:01:00.270
and she became a photojournalist, which is not something

00:01:00.270 --> 00:01:04.720
that very many people did, much less very many women did.

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:09.660
And that's rather extraordinary but one sees, even in her later work,

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:13.170
there's always an interest in the quotidian and the everyday,

00:01:13.170 --> 00:01:19.530
in what she can see in a gesture or an expression, much of which

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:24.180
undoubtedly goes back to those early years of handling photography the way

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:26.970
that many people did in middle class homes.

00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:30.820
So Beals had extraordinary range as a photographer.

00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:33.600
If you go through her photographs in the archives,

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:37.930
you discover that she took many photographs of animals.

00:01:37.930 --> 00:01:41.970
She took photographs of people doing amateur theatrical performances.

00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:43.870
She took street photography.

00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:49.140
She took ethnographic photographs at the 1904 St. Louis World's Fair.

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:51.720
She did architectural photography.

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:55.110
That range is quite unusual.

00:01:55.110 --> 00:01:59.400
A number of photographers, including some great female photographers

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:04.170
of the 19th century, were much more limited in their range.

00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:08.050
To take one example, Julia Margaret Cameron, by most accounts

00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:10.229
the greatest Victorian photographer, stuck

00:02:10.229 --> 00:02:13.630
to a very limited range of photographs.

00:02:13.630 --> 00:02:17.850
And she just went very deep in each of those genres.

00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:24.270
Whereas Beals had a much wider scope and looked into many different things

00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:27.870
to photograph, many of which we can relate to now.

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:31.950
In our moment in which cat pictures abound on the internet,

00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:35.400
it's wonderful to go through some of the photographs in the Schlesinger

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:41.790
and see her remarkable capacity to take photographs of her feline friends.

00:02:41.790 --> 00:02:48.720
It's inspiring, the extent to which one sees in the photography of Beals,

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:50.670
her self-consciousness.

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:54.750
There's many photographs in the archive that are of her.

00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:56.640
And whether they were taken by someone else

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:00.930
or whether she set up the apparatus and just had someone open and close

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:03.210
the shutter, we don't always know.

00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:07.500
But she clearly took herself and her performance in the world

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:09.510
very seriously.

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:14.430
One of my great interests in the history of photography is in its materiality,

00:03:14.430 --> 00:03:18.730
and in particular its openness to accident.

00:03:18.730 --> 00:03:23.640
This was understood by practitioners of photography from very early on, that

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:28.290
here was a way of making pictures in which chance played a role, that

00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:33.330
was different than the role it played in drawing or painting or other more

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:35.580
traditional media.

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:37.560
So whenever I'm looking at photographs, I'm

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:41.940
also thinking about how the practitioner in question

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:45.810
handled chance and these issues of materiality.

00:03:45.810 --> 00:03:50.160
There are signs in the archive that Beals was actually

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:52.500
very sensitive to these issues.

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:55.740
There's a rather marvelous picture that she

00:03:55.740 --> 00:04:00.630
took of a sitter named Leal Whitcomb Davis, who

00:04:00.630 --> 00:04:06.630
is sitting with ceramics tools in front of her on a table,

00:04:06.630 --> 00:04:09.780
before a window through which light streams.

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:13.360
And she's glazing a piece of pottery.

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:18.149
And what I love about this photograph is the self-reflexivity of it,

00:04:18.149 --> 00:04:22.440
in so far as pottery and photography have

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:25.620
some very significant commonalities.

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:28.800
First of all, you have the play of the glazing of the pot

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:31.870
and the glazing of the window through which the light streams,

00:04:31.870 --> 00:04:34.950
which is calling up the operation of photography,

00:04:34.950 --> 00:04:37.690
and the way that light streams into the camera.

00:04:37.690 --> 00:04:40.890
But also, in both photography and pottery,

00:04:40.890 --> 00:04:44.730
you apply this film to this object.

00:04:44.730 --> 00:04:47.580
You stick it in the dark, and then outcome

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:51.390
something that was, to a certain extent, unpredictable.

00:04:51.390 --> 00:04:54.210
And every potter has experienced that, and every photographer

00:04:54.210 --> 00:04:55.680
has experienced that.

00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:58.620
And I think in this beautiful, quite exquisite photograph,

00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:02.430
She brings this dialogue to the fore.

00:05:02.430 --> 00:05:05.550
One of the aspects of Beals' photography that I

00:05:05.550 --> 00:05:10.560
think is very important to register is her social conscience.

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:14.130
And there's one photograph in particular in the collection of this Schlesinger

00:05:14.130 --> 00:05:17.190
Library that really brings that home for me.

00:05:17.190 --> 00:05:22.200
It's a photograph of a dim alleyway in Washington DC,

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:25.800
with dilapidated homes on both sides.

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:31.740
But as you look down this stretch of very unappealing roadway,

00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:37.020
you see at the end of it, rising up, the dome of the US capitol,

00:05:37.020 --> 00:05:39.150
all sparkling and white.

00:05:39.150 --> 00:05:44.700
And it's that contrast between the lived reality, the material

00:05:44.700 --> 00:05:51.870
conditions of some of the more dilapidated quarters of Washington

00:05:51.870 --> 00:05:56.880
DC on the one hand, and then this symbol of national might on the other,

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:03.930
that I think clearly speaks to a social conscience about the state of affairs

00:06:03.930 --> 00:06:06.500
in the world in which she was living.

